  "text": "Allophone: A variant realization of a phoneme that does not contrast with other variants in any environment.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to a previously mentioned word or phrase.\n\nApophony: A change in a word\u2019s pronunciation that indicates grammatical information, such as tense or number.\n\nArticulatory phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced by the vocal tract.\n\nAssimilation: A phonological process where one sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nAugmentative: A type of language marked by the use of superlatives and intensifiers to express heightened meaning.\n\nAxis: A conceptual framework used in phonology to describe the position of a sound in a particular dimension.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to use two languages fluently.\n\nBilingual language acquisition: The process by which a child learns two languages simultaneously.\n\nBilingualism continuum: A model that represents the varying degrees of proficiency in two languages.\n\nBinding theory: A theory in syntax that explains how pronouns and anaphors are linked to their antecedents.\n\nCase: A grammatical category that indicates the role of a noun in a sentence, such as subject or object.\n\nCohesion: The use of linguistic devices to connect ideas and maintain text unity.\n\nComplement: A phrase or clause that completes the meaning of a verb or other head word.\n\nConcord: The agreement between a word and another word in a sentence, such as number, gender, or person.\n\nConjugation: The process of forming different verb forms for tense, mood, aspect, and person.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by obstructing the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A group of two or more consonants that appear together in a word.\n\nContrast: The ability of two speech sounds to distinguish meaning.\n\nCohesion marker: A linguistic element that links parts of a text together, such as conjunctions or pronouns.\n\nCoordination: The linking of two or more grammatical units on the same level, such as clauses or phrases.\n\nCorpus: A large collection of written or spoken texts used for linguistic analysis.\n\nDeixis: The use of words or expressions that refer to the context of time, place, or speaker.\n\nDeletion: A phonological process in which a sound is omitted in certain environments.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that involves a glide from one vowel to another.\n\nDislocation: The placement of a noun phrase outside its expected position in a sentence.\n\nDative: A grammatical case used to indicate the indirect object of a verb.\n\nDative shift: A syntactic phenomenon in which the dative object is moved to a different position in the sentence.\n\nDemonyning: The process of turning a noun into an adjective.\n\nDiacritic: A symbol added to a letter to change its pronunciation or meaning.\n\nDialect: A variety of a language that is distinguished by pronunciation, vocabulary, and grammar.\n\nDipthong: A vowel sound formed by the combination of two vowel sounds in one syllable.\n\nDiscrepancy: A difference between what is said and what is meant, often involving irony or sarcasm.\n\nDislocation: The placement of a noun phrase outside its expected syntactic position.\n\nDistributivity: The property of a linguistic element that varies according to its distribution in the language.\n\nDistributive morphology: A type of morphology in which affixes are added to indicate distributive relationships.\n\nDissimilation: A phonological process in which one sound becomes less like a neighboring sound.\n\nDowler: A person who asks for more information, typically in a clarification.\n\nDumpling: An informal term for a very small, round piece of food.\n\nDysphasia: A disorder that affects the ability",
